# Copy to .env and fill in real values:  cp .env.example .env

# --- Postgres (container) ---
POSTGRES_USER=gymapp
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-strong-password
POSTGRES_DB=gymapp

# --- Backend ---
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=change-me-long-random-secret

# Encrypts fingerprint templates before they are stored. Required — the backend
# will not start without it, whether or not a fingerprint reader is attached.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Back this up somewhere that is NOT the database backup. Losing it means every
# enrolled member has to put their finger on the reader again; keeping it beside
# the data it protects defeats the point of encrypting the data at all.
BIOMETRIC_KEY=change-me-32-byte-base64-key

# --- Host port the app is served on (http://localhost:8080) ---
APP_PORT=8080

# Set to true once the app is served over HTTPS, so the session cookie is
# marked Secure. Leave false for plain-http localhost or the browser will
# refuse to store it.
#
# The HTTPS overlay (docker-compose.https.yml) sets this for you — you only
# need to set it by hand if TLS is terminated somewhere else, by a load
# balancer or a reverse proxy in front of this stack.
HTTPS=false

# --- HTTPS (only needed with docker-compose.https.yml) ---
# The domain the certificate is issued for. See the HTTPS section of README.md.
# DOMAIN=gym.example.com

# --- Member photos on S3 ---
# Required if you want member photos at all. These containers run with
# NODE_ENV=production, where a photo upload with no bucket configured is
# refused rather than written inline: a base64 data-URL on the row is a couple
# of megabytes that every member list, every search and every backup then
# carries, and it is far easier to prevent than to migrate back out.
#
# Everything else works without them. Members, memberships, payments and
# attendance are unaffected — only adding a photo is refused, with a message
# saying which variables are missing.
#
# The bucket MUST be private. The app only ever hands out signed URLs that
# expire in minutes; a public bucket defeats that entirely. See the Member
# photos section of DOCS.md for the lockdown commands.
# AWS_REGION=ap-south-1
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_BUCKET_NAME=

# --- Google sign-in (optional) ---
# Create an OAuth 2.0 Web client at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# --- Outgoing mail (optional) ---
# Without these the app still works: invite and password-reset links are
# written to `docker compose logs backend` instead of being emailed.
#
# EASIEST: Resend — one API key, no DNS setup. https://resend.com
# Leave MAIL_FROM unset to send from onboarding@resend.dev, which needs no
# domain verification.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxx
# MAIL_FROM=Your Gym <no-reply@yourdomain.com>
#
# ALTERNATIVE: plain SMTP. Ignored when RESEND_API_KEY is set.
#
# Gmail needs an App Password, NOT your account password:
#   1. Turn on 2-Step Verification: https://myaccount.google.com/signinoptions/two-step-verification
#   2. Create an App Password:      https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
#   3. Paste the 16-character code as SMTP_PASS (spaces are fine).
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
# SMTP_PASS=abcd efgh ijkl mnop
# SMTP_FROM=Your Gym <you@gmail.com>

# The URL the browser uses. Reset links are built from it, so it must match how
# people actually reach the app — defaults to http://localhost:${APP_PORT}.
# APP_URL=http://localhost:8080

# --- Scheduled work (optional) ---
# One job: an email to each member whose membership expires that day. It reaches
# only members who have an email on their record, and does nothing until mail is
# configured above — it logs the message instead.
#
# IMPORTANT: containers run in UTC unless you say otherwise, so "9am" below is
# 9am UTC — 2pm in Karachi. Set TZ to your own zone and the schedule means what
# it looks like.
# TZ=Asia/Karachi
# REMINDER_CRON=0 9 * * *
#
# Set false to stop this container running scheduled work. Only worth doing if
# you run more than one backend, so nobody is emailed twice.
# RUN_JOBS=false
#
# A second daily job deletes the fingerprints of members who stopped coming —
# only the template, never their history. The window is 60 days to lapse plus
# the grace below, so about five months of silence by default.
# FINGERPRINT_RETENTION_CRON=0 3 * * *
# FINGERPRINT_RETENTION_GRACE_DAYS=90

# --- Database backups ---
# A sidecar container dumps the database on a loop and prunes old dumps. It is
# on by default; there is nothing to set unless you want to change it.
#
# Backups land in docker/backups on the host — deliberately not a Docker volume,
# so `docker compose down -v` cannot take the database and its backups together.
# Copy them somewhere off this machine; a backup on the same disk survives a bad
# migration, not a dead disk.
#
# BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400   # once a day
# BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS=14
#
# Restore one with ./restore.sh — it checks the archive before dropping
# anything, takes a safety dump first, and decrypts automatically:
#   ./restore.sh --list                        # what is available
#   ./restore.sh --into gymapp_check           # rehearse against a scratch db
#   ./restore.sh                               # newest dump, over the live one

# --- Backup encryption (STRONGLY recommended) ---
#
# Encrypts every dump before it is written. Without this each file in
# docker/backups is a plaintext copy of your entire member list — names,
# phones, addresses, payment history — and those files get carried onto
# laptops and USB sticks where the database itself never goes.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Keep it in a password manager, NOT on this machine and NOT beside the
# backups. Lose it and the backups cannot be restored, by you or anyone else.
# BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=change-me-long-random-passphrase

# --- Offsite backup copy (STRONGLY recommended) ---
#
# A backup that only exists on this machine is not a backup: the disk failure
# that takes the database takes the dumps beside it, and ransomware encrypts
# both in one pass. With these set, every dump is also PUT to an object store.
#
# Works with any S3-compatible provider. Backblaze B2 and Cloudflare R2 are
# the cheap options; AWS S3 works too. For AWS leave BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT unset.
#
#   B2:  https://s3.us-west-004.backblazeb2.com
#   R2:  https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#
# Use a key that can PUT but not DELETE. It sits on the same machine as the
# database, so a key that could also delete would let whoever takes the machine
# take the backups as well. Set retention with a bucket lifecycle rule rather
# than granting delete — the app never prunes offsite copies.
# BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=gym-backups
# BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
# BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=
# BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Online payments (optional) ---
# With none of this set the member portal still works: a member asking to renew
# files a request that staff confirm at the front desk. Nothing is ever marked
# paid without either a signed gateway callback or that confirmation, so a
# member can never grant themselves a membership.
#
# The public URL of the API as JazzCash/Easypaisa's servers see it. They post
# their confirmation here, so it must be reachable from the internet — behind
# localhost or a private address the callback never arrives and every payment
# stays pending. This is the one setting people get wrong.
# APP_BASE_URL=https://api.yourgym.com
#
# Where the member's browser lands after paying. Defaults to APP_URL above.
# FRONTEND_BASE_URL=https://yourgym.com
#
# JazzCash. All three are required together — you get them when your merchant
# account is approved. The integrity salt signs and verifies callbacks: it is a
# password, not an identifier. Anyone holding it can forge a payment.
# JAZZCASH_MERCHANT_ID=MC12345
# JAZZCASH_PASSWORD=abcdefghij
# JAZZCASH_INTEGRITY_SALT=your-integrity-salt
#
# Sandbox is the default in code. Switch to live only once you have tested:
# https://payments.jazzcash.com.pk/CustomerPortal/transactionmanagement/merchantform
# JAZZCASH_POST_URL=https://sandbox.jazzcash.com.pk/CustomerPortal/transactionmanagement/merchantform
#
# Easypaisa. Both required together; the hash key is likewise a password.
# EASYPAISA_STORE_ID=12345
# EASYPAISA_HASH_KEY=your-hash-key
# EASYPAISA_POST_URL=https://easypay.easypaisa.com.pk/easypay/Index.jsf

# --- Monitoring (STRONGLY recommended in production) ---
#
# Without this, a failure in production is a line in `docker compose logs` that
# nobody is reading at the moment it happens — and the container can be
# recycled before anyone thinks to look. The first report of a bug ends up
# being the gym owner's phone call.
#
# Structured request logs are written either way, to stdout. A DSN is what
# turns an error into something that *arrives*, with a stack trace and the
# request that caused it.
#
# Free tier covers 5,000 errors a month, which is far more than a gym will
# produce. Create a project at https://sentry.io (pick Node.js) and paste the
# DSN it gives you.
#
# Member data is deliberately not sent: no request bodies, no cookies, no
# headers. Only the signed-in account id and the gym id, so you can tell one
# broken gym from all of them.
# SENTRY_DSN=https://abc123@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
#
# Optional. Names the deploy a stack trace came from — a git sha works well.
# SENTRY_RELEASE=
#
# Performance tracing. Billed per transaction and off by default; the value
# here is errors. Set 0.1 to sample a tenth if latency ever becomes a question.
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0
